Write-up for PA2
----------------

Implementation overview
-----------------------

The lexical analyzer was implemented in cool.flex using Flex and follows
the lexical specification of the COOL Reference Manual and the PA2
requirements.

The scanner recognizes:

- COOL keywords, case-insensitively;
- boolean constants, respecting the special capitalization rule for
  true and false;
- TYPEID and OBJECTID identifiers;
- integer constants;
- multi-character operators (<-, <= and =>);
- single-character operators and punctuation;
- line comments;
- nested block comments;
- string constants;
- lexical errors and the required recovery behavior.

Semantic values are stored using the tables provided by the COOL
infrastructure:

- INT_CONST values are inserted in inttable;
- TYPEID and OBJECTID values are inserted in idtable;
- STR_CONST values are inserted in stringtable;
- BOOL_CONST values are stored in cool_yylval.boolean;
- lexical error descriptions are returned through
  cool_yylval.error_msg.

The scanner itself does not print lexical errors. Errors are returned as
ERROR tokens.


Comments
--------

Line comments beginning with -- are ignored until a newline or EOF.

Block comments use an exclusive Flex state named COMMENT. A nesting
counter is maintained so that nested comments are accepted. The scanner
returns to INITIAL only when the nesting depth becomes zero.

Newlines inside block comments update curr_lineno.

EOF inside an open block comment returns:

    EOF in comment

A block-comment terminator appearing outside a block comment returns:

    Unmatched *)


Strings
-------

String constants are handled using an exclusive STRING state.

Characters are accumulated in the supplied string buffer and the final
value is inserted into stringtable.

The following special escapes are converted before being stored:

    \n
    \t
    \b
    \f

For other escaped characters, the escaped character itself is stored.
Therefore, for example, the textual sequence \0 is valid and produces
the character '0'. It is different from an actual NUL byte.

A backslash followed by a physical newline is accepted. The newline is
stored in the string value and curr_lineno is incremented.

An unescaped physical newline terminates the invalid string and returns:

    Unterminated string constant

EOF before the closing quote returns:

    EOF in string constant

COOL strings are limited to 1024 characters. The implementation reserves
one additional byte for the terminating C NUL character. A value longer
than 1024 characters returns:

    String constant too long

An actual NUL byte inside a string returns:

    String contains null character


String error recovery
---------------------

A separate exclusive Flex state, STRING_RECOVERY, is used after errors
such as an oversized string or a string containing a NUL byte.

After the ERROR token has been returned, the scanner continues consuming
the invalid string until its logical end. Escaped quotes do not
prematurely terminate recovery, escaped physical newlines update
curr_lineno, and an ordinary physical newline terminates the invalid
string.

This prevents the remaining contents of an invalid string from being
incorrectly interpreted as normal COOL tokens.

If EOF is reached during recovery, no second lexical error is generated,
because the original string error has already been reported.


Rule ordering
-------------

The scanner relies on Flex longest-match behavior together with explicit
rule ordering.

Multi-character operators are recognized before their single-character
components. Keywords and boolean constants have explicit rules, while
the more general identifier rules ensure that longer identifiers such
as classx, whilex and trueValue are not split into shorter tokens.

The final rule in the INITIAL state reports an otherwise unrecognized
character as an ERROR token containing that character and allows
scanning to continue at the following character.


Line counting
-------------

curr_lineno is incremented whenever a physical newline is consumed in a
context in which that newline belongs to the input stream, including:

- normal source text;
- block comments;
- escaped physical newlines in valid strings;
- physical newlines encountered during string recovery;
- unterminated strings terminated by an unescaped newline.


Testing
-------

The supplied test.cl was expanded to exercise representative cases from
all major lexical categories, including:

- keywords and capitalization;
- boolean constants;
- TYPEID and OBJECTID;
- integer constants;
- operators and punctuation;
- longest-match boundaries;
- line comments;
- nested block comments;
- valid strings;
- escape sequences;
- escaped physical newlines;
- invalid characters;
- unmatched comment terminators;
- unterminated strings;
- interactions between strings, comments and normal tokens.

Additional development tests were kept in the tests/ directory to cover
cases that cannot conveniently coexist in one test.cl or require binary
input. These include:

- EOF inside strings;
- EOF inside block comments;
- EOF inside nested comments;
- strings of exactly 1024 characters;
- strings exceeding 1024 characters;
- actual NUL bytes inside strings;
- recovery after string errors;
- escaped quotes during recovery;
- escaped newlines during recovery;
- EOF during recovery;
- integration of Core, Comments and Strings.

Regression testing was performed after implementing each lexical area
and again after integration. The complete scanner was also tested with
mixed valid input and with multiple recoverable errors in the same file.


Correctness
-----------

The scanner was validated against the required token classes, semantic
values, lexical error messages, recovery rules and line-number behavior.

In particular, tests confirmed that:

- comments do not produce tokens;
- comment delimiters inside strings remain part of the string;
- quotes and token-like text inside comments are ignored;
- invalid characters generate one ERROR and scanning continues;
- nested block comments are handled correctly;
- string errors do not cause their remaining contents to be tokenized;
- valid input following recoverable lexical errors is still recognized;
- special identifiers such as self and SELF_TYPE receive no special
  lexical treatment beyond the normal OBJECTID/TYPEID rules;
- the scanner does not perform integer-overflow checking.
